ent different Q1 values with corresponding Q2 and Q3/Q1 ratios. This is a multi-dimensional matrix: variant × orientation → list of (Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q3/Q1) tuples.\n\nGiven this complexity, the best representation:\n\n- `model_family` with models identified by combination, e.g. \"ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5\" etc. Hmm, but each heading covers multiple ET types.\n\nActually each bold heading is a variant group: e.g. \"ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q₃ 2,5 m³/h\" covers ET1-ET4 at DN20 with Q3=2.5. Then the same heading appears twice: once with Orientation H and once with rising/falling/V orientations. Each table lists multiple Q1 options (columns).\n\nSo the matrix dimension per variant group: columns = Q3/Q1 ratio options (16, 20, 25, ...). For each column: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q2/Q1, Q3/Q1.\n\nHow to structure? Options:\n\n1. `matrix_tables[]` — one entry per table, with name indicating the variant and orientation, columns = q1, q2, q3, q4, q2_over_q1, q3_over_q1, rows per column index (or per ratio). That preserves data faithfully.\n\n2. `characteristics.config_level[]` — attribute × condition.\n\nGiven the schema supports `matrix_tables` with `rows[]` object per row, I'll emit one matrix table per heading block (variant + orientation), with rows keyed by q3_over_q1 ratio or by column index. Each row: {q1, q2, q3, q4, q2_over_q1, q3_over_q1}.\n\nBut wait — the units: q1..q4 in m³/h, ratios dimensionless. The schema example includes \"column_units\".\n\nAlso there's a leading partial table at the very top (Q1 0,100/0,080; Q3 1,6; Q4 2,00; ±2%; temp 0,1–30°C; orientation rising/falling/V). That's the tail of a variant from page 5 (probably DN15 or DN20, Q3 1,6 with cold water — actually it says accuracy ±2% which typically cold; temperature 0,1–30°C = cold water). We don't know the variant heading for it (it's in part 1). Since this fragment is part 2, I should include it as a matrix table with unknown name — hmm. The instruction: \"for the content present in THIS fragment on