20; Q3 4.0; cold; ±2%/±5%; H; ratios 25–80\nM4: same; vertical; ratios 25–40\nM5: ET1,ET3,ET4; DN15; Q3 2.5; hot 30–90°C; ±3%/±5%; H; ratios 25–100\nM6: same; vertical; ratios 25–50\nM7: ET2; DN15; Q3 2.5; hot; ±3%/±5%; H; ratios 25–80\nM8: same; vertical; ratios 25–31.5\nM9: ET1,ET3,ET4; DN15; Q3 1.6; hot; ±3%/±5%; H; ratios 20–80\nM0 (partial): Q3 1.6; cold 0.1–30°C; ±2%/±5%; vertical; ratios 16, 20; ET/DN unknown in this fragment.\n\nFor model_id, I'll use e.g. \"ET1-ET4_DN20_Q3-2.5_H\"? Or \"DN20 Q3 2,5 m³/h (ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4), H\". Let me use: \"ET1,ET2,ET3,ET4 DN20 Q3 2.5 H\". Hmm.\n\nActually simpler and stable: model_id strings like \"DN20_Q3-2.5_ET1234_H\". I'll go with readable: \"ET1+ET2+ET3+ET4_DN20_Q3-2.5_H\". Eh. Let me pick: \"ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4, DN20, Q3 2,5 m³/h, H\" — mirrors the certificate heading plus orientation. That's clear. But for partial: \"unknown variant, Q3 1,6 m³/h, vertical (from fragment start)\".\n\nHmm, for the partial one, maybe better to just include its matrix table with description noting the variant heading is in the previous fragment, and NOT create a model entry (since we can't key it). But then matrix table rows reference... it's fine, matrix_tables are standalone.\n\nActually, I'll create a model entry too with model_id \"ET?,DN?,Q3-1.6_cold_V\" — no, too fabricated. I'll include it in matrix_tables only, with name \"Flow range (variant heading not in this fragment)\". And describe attributes in description: Q3 1.6 m³/h, cold water, class 2, vertical orientation.\n\nHmm wait, let me reconsider: The heading right above the partial table (in part 1) would tell us the variant. Since it's cut, best-effort: include as separate matrix table with description. Good.\n\nNow matrix_tables: one per block = 10 tables. Columns: [\"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q2_over_q1\", \"q3_over_q1\"], column_units: [\"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null]. Rows: one row per column-of-the-original-table (i.e., per flow-rate option), keyed by... the rows need a key? In the R137 example, rows have \"size\" as key. Here there's no natural row key other than index or ratio. I'll key each row by \"q3_over_q1\" implicitly — rows are objects with all fields including q3_over_q1. That's fine.\n\nAlternatively transpose: rows per attribute. The original table is transposed (attributes as rows, options as columns). I'll emit rows per option: {\"q1\": 0.16, \"q2\": 0.25, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"q3_over_q1\": 16}.\n\nNumbers: 0,16 → 0.16; 0,13 → 0.13; 0,10 → 0.10 → 0.1; 0,08 → 0.08; 0,06 → 0.06; 0,050 → 0.05; 0,05 → 0.05; 31,5 → 31.5.\n\nKeep trailing precision as JSON numbers (0.10 becomes 0.1; fine).\n\nTable names: e.g. \"ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 2,5 m³/h — orientation H: flow range\". \n\nNow `characteristics.model_level`: which attributes vary across models? q3 (1.6, 2.5, 4.0), q4 (2.00, 3.13, 5.00), water temperature range (0.1–30 vs 30–90), accuracy high-zone (±2% vs ±3%), nominal diameter (DN15/DN20), orientation (H vs vertical). I'll add model_level entries for:\n\n- q3: unit m³/h, values per model\n- q4: unit m³/h, values per model\n- water_temperature_range: unit °C, values {min,max} per model\n- Maybe skip diameter/orientation since they're in model attributes.\n\nHmm, that duplicates model attributes. The R137 example does both (model attributes minimal, model_level for flow rates). I'll do: model_level for q3 and q4 and water_temperature_range. And keep orientation/dn/et-types in model attributes.\n\nWait, but for the partial table, no model entry... its q3=1.6 cold. I'll skip it in model_level (only the 9 defined models). Hmm, or include an entry \"variant from previous fragment\". Skip.\n\nAlso `characteristics.type_level`: anything constant across all? Q2/Q1 = 1.6 always — constant across the whole cert. Could add type_level: q2_over_q1 = 1.6. That's true for all tables including partial. I'll add:\n- \"q2_over_q1\": {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: null}\nAlso temperature ranges: accuracy limits: \"maximum_permisible_error_high_zone\": ±2% or ±3% varies per model → model_level? It correlates with temperature. I'll put accuracy in model attributes (\"accuracy_class\": \"±2 % (Q2≤Q≤Q4), ±5 % (Q1≤Q<Q2)\"). Hmm, or accuracy_classes in model attributes as 2 or 3. I'll put \"accuracy_class\": 2 / 3 (R49 class numbers)... risky. Put the stated tolerances: {\"accuracy_high_zone\": \"±2%\", \"accuracy_low_zone\": \"±5%\"}. Hmm keep simple: attributes: {\"accuracy\": \"±2 % (Q₂ ≤ Q ≤ Q₄); ±5 % (Q₁ ≤ Q < Q₂)\"}.\n\nLet me not over-engineer. Decide final structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2006-DE1-14.01\",\n    \"page_total\": 11\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"ET1\", \"ET2\", \"ET3\", \"ET4\"],\n    \"description\": \"Water meters types ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4 in DN15 and DN20 with Q3 1.6 / 2.5 / 4.0 m³/h...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {...},\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\"q2_over_q1\": {...}},\n    \"model_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2006, \"accuracy_classes\": [2, 3]}\n}\n```\n\nHmm, page_total — \"Page 8 of 11 pages\" — yes 11.\n\nWait, should category be \"Water meter\" or \"Gas meter\"? R49 = water meters intended for metering of cold potable water and hot potable water. The certificate number R49 confirms. Category: \"Water meter\". Good.\n\nmodel_family.family_name: not stated in fragment → null (schema says include family_name if any else null). Actually should I include model_family only if >1 model? Yes, there are many variants. Include.\n\nModels array. Let me write them:\n\n```json\n{\"model_id\": \"ET1,ET2,ET3,ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5 H\", \"label\": \"ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 2,5 m³/h, orientation H\", \"attributes\": {\n  \"flow_sensor_types\": [\"ET1\",\"ET2\",\"ET3\",\"ET4\"],\n  \"nominal_diameter\": {\"value\": \"DN20\", ...},\n  ...\n}}\n```\n\nHmm, attributes as structured values would be consistent. But schema example shows attributes{} free-form. I'll use structured values where numeric: q3: {\"value\": 2.5, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"unit_id\": null}. Nominal diameter: {\"value\": \"DN20\"} or just \"DN20\". Keep it simple: strings for DN.\n\nModel ids — let me finalize a scheme: \"ET1-4_DN20_Q3-2.5_H\" etc. Hmm, \"ET1-4\" is ambiguous. Use \"ET1|ET2|ET3|ET4\"? Pipe chars in ids fine but ugly. I'll use commas: \"ET1,ET2,ET3,ET4_DN20_Q3-2.5_H\". Eh.\n\nActually simpler: model_id: \"DN20_Q3-2.5_H_ET1-ET4\". Let me just do: \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3 2.5 H\" where \"ET1-ET4\" means ET1 through ET4. For M5/M6: \"ET1/ET3/ET4 DN15 Q3 2.5 H\". For M7/M8: \"ET2 DN15 Q3 2.5 H\". M9: \"ET1/ET3/ET4 DN15 Q3 1.6 H\".\n\nFine. I'll use \"+\" separator: \"ET1+ET2+ET3+ET4_DN20_Q3-2.5_H\". I'll go with that? Hmm... readable enough. Actually let me use spaces: \"ET1 ET2 ET3 ET4 DN20 Q3 2.5 H\". Meh. Decide: model_id = \"ET1+ET2+ET3+ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5 H\". OK whatever, pick one and be consistent: I'll use underscores between fields: \n\n- \"ET1+ET2+ET3+ET4_DN20_Q3-2.5_H\"\n- \"ET1+ET2+ET3+ET4_DN20_Q3-2.5_V\"\n- \"ET1+ET2+ET3+ET4_DN20_Q3-4.0_H\"\n- \"ET1+ET2+ET3+ET4_DN20_Q3-4.0_V\"\n- \"ET1+ET3+ET4_DN15_Q3-2.5_H\"\n- \"ET1+ET3+ET4_DN15_Q3-2.5_V\"\n- \"ET2_DN15_Q3-2.5_H\"\n- \"ET2_DN15_Q3-2.5_V\"\n- \"ET1+ET3+ET4_DN15_Q3-1.6_H\"\n\nWhere V = vertical orientations (rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° / V 45°), H = horizontal.\n\nLabels: full descriptive: \"ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 2,5 m³/h — orientation H (horizontal)\".\n\nAttributes per model:\n- \"flow_sensor_types\": [\"ET1\",\"ET2\",\"ET3\",\"ET4\"]\n- \"nominal_diameter\": \"DN20\"\n- \"q3\": {\"value\": 2.5, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"unit_id\": null}\n- \"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 30}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n- \"accuracy\": {\"value\": \"±2 % (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4); ±5 % (Q1 ≤ Q < Q2)\"} — hmm as string value. Or accuracy_high_zone / low_zone with unit \"%\". I'll do:\n  - \"maximum permissible error (Q2≤Q≤Q4)\": {\"value\": 2, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"} → canonical label \"maximum_permisible_error_high_zone\" (spell permissible correctly: \"maximum_permissible_error_high_zone\")\n  - \"maximum_permissible_error_low_zone\": {\"value\": 5, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n- \"orientation\": {\"value\": \"H\"} or \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° / V 45°\"\n- \"q3_over_q1_options\": [16,20,25,31.5,40,50]? That's in matrix table; skip in attributes to avoid duplication? Could include; I'll skip since matrix_tables cover it.\n\nHmm wait, \"Orientation: H\" — in R49 H = horizontal. Keep raw \"H\".\n\nNow matrix_tables. 10 tables. Each:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Flow range — ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 2,5 m³/h, orientation H\",\n  \"columns\": [\"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q2_over_q1\", \"q3_over_q1\"],\n  \"column_units\": [\"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"q1\": 0.16, \"q2\": 0.25, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"q3_over_q1\": 16},\n    {\"q1\": 0.13, \"q2\": 0.20, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"q3_over_q1\": 20},\n    {\"q1\": 0.10, \"q2\": 0.16, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"q3_over_q1\": 25},\n    {\"q1\": 0.08, \"q2\": 0.13, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"q3_over_q1\": 31.5},\n    {\"q1\": 0.06, \"q2\": 0.10, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"q3_over_q1\": 40},\n    {\"q1\": 0.050, \"q2\": 0.080, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"q3_over_q1\": 50}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n0.20 → 0.2 in JSON. Fine.\n\nFor block 3 (Q3 4.0 H): Q1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08, 0.06, 0.05; Q2: 0.26, 0.20, 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08; ratios 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80.\n\nBlock 5 (DN15 Q3 2.5 H hot): Q1: 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040, 0.031, 0.025; Q2: 0.160, 0.127, 0.100, 0.080, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040; ratios 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80, 100.\n\nBlock 9 (DN15 Q3 1.6 H hot): Q1: 0.080, 0.064, 0.051, 0.040, 0.032, 0.025, 0.020; Q2: 0.128, 0.102, 0.081, 0.064, 0.051, 0.041, 0.032; ratios 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80.\n\nPartial table: rows: {q1: 0.100, q2: 0.160, q3: 1.6, q4: 2.00, ratio 16}, {q1: 0.080, q2: 0.128, q3: 1.6, q4: 2.00, ratio 20}.\n\nNote block 9 Q2 at ratio 31.5: 0.081 — keep 0.081. At ratio 63: 0.041 (1.6×0.025... wait Q1=0.025, ×1.6=0.04, but table says 0,041 — keep as printed 0.041). Yes, transcribe as printed.\n\nBlock 5: Q2 at ratio 31.5: 0,127 (0.079×1.6=0.1264→0.127 as printed). Keep printed values.\n\nAlso description for each matrix table: mention accuracy class and water temperature? The config info (accuracy, temp, orientation) applies to the table; I'll put in description: \"Accuracy class: ±2 % (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4), ±5 % (Q1 ≤ Q < Q2). Water temperature range 0.1–30 °C.\"\n\nBut should accuracy/temp also go into config_level? Schema: characteristics.config_level for attribute × config-axis. Orientation is a config axis: e.g. attribute \"maximum flow rate ratio range\"... Overkill. The model entries already encode orientation (H/V as separate models). I think model split + matrix tables is enough. Alternatively use config_level with axis \"orientation\":\n\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"q3_over_q1_options\", \"axis\": \"orientation\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"H\", \"value\": [16,20,25,31.5,40,50]}, {\"condition\": \"V\", \"value\": [16,20,25]}]}\n```\nThat's a nice representation but duplicates matrix tables. I'll include maybe one config_level for minimum flow rate? Hmm. Let me add config_level entries for q1 (minimum flow rate) varying by orientation:\n\nFor DN20 Q3 2.5: H: q1 ∈ {0.16...0.05}; V: q1 ∈ {0.16, 0.13, 0.10}. As lists — not great structured form.\n\nI'll skip config_level to keep clean; matrix_tables carry it. Actually the schema rule 5 says use config_level when attributes vary by configuration. Here orientation is treated as separate model variants in my design, which is also acceptable (\"H\" and \"V\" as distinct models). Hmm, but they're the same meter installed in different orientations — arguably a config axis, not separate models. The certificate headings repeat identical headings (same ET/DN/Q3) differing only in orientation, which suggests orientation is a configuration of the same model. \n\nBetter design: models keyed by (ET-set, DN, Q3, temperature) — 6 models:\n- \"ET1+ET2+ET3+ET4_DN20_Q3-2.5_cold\"\n- \"ET1+ET2+ET3+ET4_DN20_Q3-4.0_cold\"\n- \"ET1+ET3+ET4_DN15_Q3-2.5_hot\"\n- \"ET2_DN15_Q3-2.5_hot\"\n- \"ET1+ET3+ET4_DN15_Q3-1.6_hot\"\n- (partial: Q3-1.6 cold, unknown ET/DN)\n\nAnd config_level with axis \"orientation\":\n- attribute q1 (minimum flow rate), axis \"orientation\", values per orientation with lists? Hmm, config_level values: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. value could be an object? For q1 range across ratios it's multiple values.\n\nHonestly, the matrix_tables already fully capture the orientation dimension (one table per heading+orientation). For config_level I could record, e.g., attribute \"q3_over_q1\" axis \"orientation\":\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"q3_over_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"orientation\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"H (horizontal)\", \"value\": [16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50]},\n    {\"condition\": \"vertical (rising and falling pipe / V 90° / V 45°)\", \"value\": [16, 20, 25]}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nBut that's per model; config_level entries don't have a model key... The schema: config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}. No model dimension — so it only works if there's a single config axis globally, which isn't the case here (varies by model). So config_level is a poor fit globally. Keep orientation in models. Fine — treat each heading+orientation as a model variant. That's defensible: \"the cert covers model variants\".\n\nSo 9 models + partial. For the partial, I'll create model? Its heading is missing. I'll skip model entry, include matrix table with description.\n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider whether to even include the partial table. Rule: \"Return ... for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" The partial table IS present. Include it. Name: \"Flow range — (variant heading in preceding fragment), Q3 1,6 m³/h, orientation vertical\". Description notes accuracy ±2%, temp 0.1–30 °C.\n\nNow also `characteristics.model_level`: with 9 models, q3 values:\n- ET1+...+ET4_DN20_Q3-2.5_H: 2.5\n- ..._V: 2.5\n- ..._Q3-4.0_H: 4.0, etc.\n\nI'll emit:\n- attribute \"q3\", unit m³/h, 9 values\n- attribute \"q4\", unit m³/h, 9 values (2.5→3.13; 4.0→5.00; 1.6→2.00)\n- attribute \"water_temperature_range\", unit °C: {min:0.1,max:30} for DN20 models; {min:30,max:90} for DN15 hot models.\n- attribute \"maximum_permissible_error_Q2_to_Q4\": 2% or 3%.\n\nGood. unit_id: null everywhere (unitsdb uncertain; m³/h might be \"u:cubic_metre_per_hour\" but rule says leave null unless certain — I'm fairly certain of u:degree_Celsius and maybe u:cubic_metre_per_hour... but I'll leave null to be safe, except degree Celsius which the spec example gives: \"u:degree_Celsius\". I'll set unit_id \"u:degree_Celsius\" for temperature. For m³/h, I'll guess null. Actually the example in rule 3 shows unit_id \"u:degree_Celsius\" as a canonical example, so use it where certain.)\n\nq2_over_q1 type_level: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}.\n\nAlso, should I include a \"flow_range\" description in certified_type.description? Yes: \"Water meters types ET1–ET4, DN15 and DN20, Q3 = 1.6 / 2.5 / 4.0 m³/h, cold water (0.1–30 °C) and hot water (30–90 °C), horizontal and vertical installations.\" That's a synthesis — acceptable as description.\n\nCareful about no LaTeX, no footnote markers — there are none here.\n\nDecimal conversion: all commas → dots. ✓.\n\nNow, \"Q₃ 2,5 m³/h\" in model labels — I'll normalize to \"Q3 2.5 m³/h\".\n\nLet me also double check block 1 Q1 values: \"0,16\", \"0,13\", \"0,10\", \"0,08\", \"0,06\", \"0,050\" → 0.16, 0.13, 0.1, 0.08, 0.06, 0.05. Q2: 0.25, 0.20, 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.080 → 0.25, 0.2, 0.16, 0.13, 0.1, 0.08. Ratios 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50. ✓\n\nBlock 3 Q1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08, 0.06, 0.05; Q2: 0.26, 0.20, 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08; ratios 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80. ✓ (0.26 as printed — note Q2/Q1 ratio column says 1.6 for all.)\n\nBlock 5 Q1: 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040, 0.031, 0.025; Q2: 0.160, 0.127, 0.100, 0.080, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040; ratios 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80, 100. ✓\n\nBlock 7 Q1: 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040, 0.031; Q2: 0.160, 0.127, 0.100, 0.080, 0.063, 0.050; ratios 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80. ✓\n\nBlock 8: Q1 0.100, 0.079; Q2 0.160, 0.127; ratios 25, 31.5. ✓\n\nBlock 9: Q1 0.080, 0.064, 0.051, 0.040, 0.032, 0.025, 0.020; Q2 0.128, 0.102, 0.081, 0.064, 0.051, 0.041, 0.032; ratios 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80. ✓\n\nBlock 2 (V): Q1 0.16, 0.13, 0.10; Q2 0.25, 0.20, 0.16; ratios 16, 20, 25. ✓\n\nBlock 4 (V): Q1 0.16, 0.13, 0.10; Q2 0.26, 0.20, 0.16; ratios 25, 31.5, 40. ✓\n\nBlock 6 (V): Q1 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050; Q2 0.160, 0.127, 0.100, 0.080; ratios 25, 31.5, 40, 50. ✓\n\nQ3/Q4 per block:\n- DN20 Q3 2.5 blocks: q3 2.5, q4 3.13\n- DN20 Q3 4.0 blocks: q3 4.0, q4 5.00\n- DN15 Q3 2.5 hot blocks: q3 2.5, q4 3.13\n- DN15 Q3 1.6 hot: q3 1.6, q4 2.00\n- Partial: q3 1.6, q4 2.00\n\nNow output size: 10 matrix tables with rows — that's fine.\n\nShould I include \"project_number\", \"scheme\", \"member_state\", \"date_issued\", \"oiml_issuer_id\" for certificate? Not present in this fragment (member_state maybe \"DE\" implied by \"DE1\" in cert number — that's the PTB country code; I could set member_state: \"Germany\"? The cert number \"R49/2006-DE1-14.01\" contains DE1. Hmm, rule 8: don't fabricate. The PTB is the German NMI; issuing_authority name given. member_state: \"DE\" is inferable but I'll omit to be safe. Actually, DE1 is literally in the certificate number — member_state \"Germany\" is safe enough? I'll omit; the authority name conveys it.\n\nIssuing authority: name only (\"Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt\"); address/person not in fragment → omit those fields.\n\nTest reports, revision history, footnotes: not present → omit.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2006. accuracy_classes: [2, 3]? Let me think again. The text \"Accuracy class: ± 2 % (Q₂ ≤ Q ≤ Q₄)\" — hmm, in R49-1 the accuracy classes for water meters: cold water meters have classes 1 and 2; hot water meters classes 1, 2, 3. Class 2: high zone ±2% (cold), low ±5%. Hot class 3: ±3% / ±5%. The cert's ±2% cold = class 2; ±3% hot = class 3. I'm fairly confident. But to minimize fabrication risk, I could instead record the MPEs per model and put accuracy_classes: [2, 3]. I'll include it — it's standard and derivable. Actually hmm, hot class 3 high zone: is it ±3%? R49-1:2006 4.2.2.1: maximum permissible error high zones: class 1: ±1% (cold)/... ; for hot water meters class 3: ±3%. Yes, hot classes are 1, 2, 3 with high-zone ±1/±2/±3%. So ±3% hot = class 3. And ±2% cold = class 2. Include accuracy_classes: [2, 3].\n\nmodel_family.family_name: The fragment doesn't state a family name. Schema: \"explicit family name if any, else null\" → null.\n\nOne more consideration: should model entries include \"label\"? Optional. I'll include label = the certificate heading text + orientation.\n\nLet me also decide on attributes keys (snake_case): flow_sensor_types (array of strings), nominal_diameter (string \"DN20\"), q3 (structured), q4 (structured), water_temperature_range (structured), maximum_permissible_error_high_zone (structured, %), maximum_permissible_error_low_zone (structured, %), orientation (structured string), q3_over_q1_options? skip.\n\nActually for DN20 blocks accuracy ±2%: high zone = Q2≤Q≤Q4 ±2%; low zone = Q1≤Q<Q2 ±5%. Hot DN15: ±3% / ±5%.\n\nNow model_level characteristics: \n\n```json\n[\n  {\"attribute\": \"q3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"unit_id\": null, \"va